WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.006
Last week, Anthropic published a guide to building always-on agents.

2
00:00:05.006 --> 00:00:10.154
Agents that sleep until something happens, do the job, and go back to sleep.

3
00:00:10.154 --> 00:00:13.608
The guide explains the pattern with clear diagrams.

4
00:00:13.608 --> 00:00:15.166
I wanted to see it run.

5
00:00:15.166 --> 00:00:18.350
So I built it, Cloudflare style, and it's live.

6
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.995
Every piece of the guide lands on something Cloudflare already ships.

7
00:00:24.995 --> 00:00:31.846
The inbox is a Durable Object for each user, so the same email sent twice only counts once.

8
00:00:31.846 --> 00:00:35.083
Memory is that Durable Object's own SQLite.

9
00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:37.568
Timers are Durable Object alarms.

10
00:00:37.568 --> 00:00:40.805
Permissions pause the work and end the run.

11
00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:43.440
And the outbox is the only way out.

12
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:46.549
Here's my favorite part.

13
00:00:46.549 --> 00:00:51.317
Ask for a refund, and the agent fills in the form and goes to sleep.

14
00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.350
It doesn't sit there waiting.

15
00:00:53.350 --> 00:01:01.342
When you sign, an hour or a week later, it wakes up right where it left off, and the refund goes out exactly once.

16
00:01:01.342 --> 00:01:04.357
Say no, and it tells the customer that too.

17
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.798
Every letter gets a number, so you can follow it across the desk.

18
00:01:09.798 --> 00:01:13.612
Send the same letter twice, and the copy is stamped duplicate.

19
00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:18.103
Ask for a reminder, and an alarm wakes the agent with a brand new letter.

20
00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:22.470
Tap the chat bubble to see everything it did while you weren't looking.

21
00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:32.028
Under the hood, it's Cloudflare's Think agent harness on Durable Objects and Workflows, with Claude Haiku doing the thinking.

22
00:01:32.028 --> 00:01:34.748
The demo and the code are in the comments.

23
00:01:34.748 --> 00:01:39.605
Thank you, William Hu, Rodrigo Olivares, and Michael Segner, for the guide.

24
00:01:39.605 --> 00:01:42.843
What would you have your always-on agent do first?
